Bundle-Name: Model Atlas Compliance Analysis
Bundle-Description: What every static compliance analyser needs and none of them should own: which \
 stages a reviewed model may be found in, which review of it applies, what that review says about \
 a feature, the text a requirement cites when the analyser has a statement of its own to evidence, \
 and how a finding becomes a Diagnostic. Pure functions over the compliance models and the scope \
 topology - no OSGi components, no language.

# Extracted from org.eclipse.fennec.model.atlas.qvt.gdpr (issue #365), where these classes were
# package-private because there was one analyser. There are about to be several, and a second copy
# of the stage ladder is a second chance for two analysers to disagree about which review applies
# to the same model - a disagreement nothing would report, because each would be internally
# consistent.
#
# The line this bundle draws: everything here is true of any artefact that is analysed against a
# metamodel review. What a particular artefact's flows are, which rules fire on them and what
# sentences they are written in stays with that artefact's analyser - those are the parts that
# differ, and the parts a reviewer of one language must be able to read on their own.
#
# No DS components on purpose. An analyser binds the services; this bundle is what it calls.
# ComplianceContexts (issue #366) reads a registry and is still not a component: an analyser
# already binds a scope service and already has a configuration to name its registries, so giving
# this one of its own would add a PID to every deployment and a service whose target, left unset,
# binds nothing and resolves nothing while the analyser carries on as though the context simply
# had no such requirement.

-workingset: ModelAtlas

-library: enableEMF

-buildpath: \
	org.eclipse.fennec.compliance.report.model;version=latest,\
	org.eclipse.fennec.compliance.context.model;version=latest,\
	org.eclipse.fennec.compliance.corpus.model;version=latest,\
	org.eclipse.fennec.model.atlas.scope.api;version=snapshot,\
	org.eclipse.fennec.model.atlas.management;version=snapshot

# junit-jupiter-api alone, as the sibling gdpr bundles have it: the engine and the platform
# launcher come from the workspace build.gradle, and bnd's copies here as well would mix two
# versions of the platform, at which point the engine discovers no tests at all. Nothing else is
# needed: the fixtures are built in code, so no test reads a file.
-testpath: junit-jupiter-api

javac.source: 21
javac.target: 21

Bundle-Version: 1.0.0.SNAPSHOT
